Ask the machine whether its server is up until the answer settles.

The engine starts a machine, the machine starts an NFS server, and the engine
then opens a connection to it to be sure. The connection is attempted once. Any
error at all -- including one that arrives in milliseconds because the bridge
carrying it is not up yet -- is taken as the server having failed.

"No route to host" is exactly that error. It is not a timeout, so the ten
seconds the connection is given never apply: the attempt fails immediately, on
a machine whose log says READY AND WAITING FOR NFS CLIENT CONNECTIONS, and
whose address answers a ping without loss a moment later.

Whoever asked for the drive then waited, watching a progress bar that had
stopped moving, while nothing was trying. Measured on this Mac: minutes of it,
before the attempt was given up on and the one after opened the drive.

So it is asked until it settles: for half a minute, a quarter of a second
apart, and only then given up on. A server that is genuinely not there costs
the same half minute it always cost.

--- a/anylinuxfs/src/cmd_mount.rs
+++ b/anylinuxfs/src/cmd_mount.rs
@@ -87,14 +87,37 @@
             .context("Failed to resolve VM host address")?;
         host_println!("Checking NFS server on {:?}...", addr);
 
-        match TcpStream::connect_timeout(&addr, Duration::from_secs(10)) {
-            Ok(_) => {
-                return Ok(nfs_ready);
-            }
-            Err(e) => {
-                host_eprintln!("Error connecting to port {}: {}", port, e);
-                return Ok(NfsStatus::Failed(None));
+        // Asked until the answer settles, not once.
+        //
+        // The machine has just started and the bridge carrying this connection
+        // comes up a moment after it. "No route to host" arrives in
+        // milliseconds -- it is not a timeout, so the ten seconds above never
+        // apply -- and returning Failed on it gave up on a server that was
+        // already printing READY AND WAITING FOR NFS CLIENT CONNECTIONS. The
+        // same address answered a ping without loss immediately afterwards.
+        //
+        // Whoever asked then waited for their drive while nothing was trying.
+        let deadline = Instant::now() + Duration::from_secs(30);
+        let mut last: Option<std::io::Error> = None;
+        loop {
+            match TcpStream::connect_timeout(&addr, Duration::from_secs(10)) {
+                Ok(_) => {
+                    return Ok(nfs_ready);
+                }
+                Err(e) => {
+                    if Instant::now() >= deadline {
+                        host_eprintln!("Error connecting to port {}: {}", port, e);
+                        return Ok(NfsStatus::Failed(None));
+                    }
+                    last = Some(e);
+                    std::thread::sleep(Duration::from_millis(250));
+                }
             }
+        }
+        #[allow(unreachable_code)]
+        {
+            let _ = last;
+            unreachable!()
         }
     }
 
